Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 06:23:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 170/178] feat(gastown): ship the polecat claim as an
 executable the prompt calls (gcp-zzb0)

Every polecat session opened by retyping a 267-line GC_CLAIM heredoc as one
Bash call: about 4.5k output tokens and 20-60s of wall time per session, and
a copy of race-critical logic that only ever ran inside a model's transcription
of it. The claim now lives in gastown/assets/scripts/polecat-claim.sh and the
prompt carries a five-line call to it.

The script takes the binding prefix as its one required argument and carries
the heredoc body byte for byte, with the four {{ .BindingPrefix }} values
becoming ${BINDING_PREFIX}. The prompt finds the script through
{{ .ConfigDir }} (gascity #5315, in v1.5.0). On an older gc that renders empty,
the -f test fails and the block prints CLAIM_REJECTED and drains, so it fails
closed rather than claiming wrongly.

Every reader of the heredoc moves in the same commit: the live-owner guard and
resume re-verify tests extract the guard from the script (resume-reverify stays
on the prompt), the pack-asset pins read the script, and the bare-bd lint row
follows the re-pointed fixture. test_polecat_claim.sh runs the real script
against a stubbed gc and sleep, one test per claim path (27) plus the
missing-argument rejection, asserting stdout and the exact call log.

Validation: the identity harness shows 27 same / 0 differ against the merge
base for prefix "gastown." and for "". Two can-fail mutations (worker-check
pattern, hook-retry sleep) turn test_case_livepole and test_case_hookfail red.
Behaviour is identical on purpose; the two comment/code mismatches found on
the way are gcp-4b7ji.

